  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/la-borda/', label: 'La Borda, Barcelona' }}
  next={{ href: '/spreefeld/', label: 'Spreefeld, Berlin' }}
>
  <section class="case-reading mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-6 pt-12 sm:px-12">
    <p>Kalkbreite puts cooperative housing and commercial uses around and above a working Zürich tram depot. Opened in 2014, it accommodates about 250 residents. The cooperative describes a roughly 2,500 m² courtyard open to the public and about 1,500 m² of roof terraces for residents. <a class="source-link" href="https://www.kalkbreite.net/bauten/kalkbreite">[Project source]</a></p>
    <p>The distinction matters: a publicly accessible court and a resident terrace have different jobs. They need different access arrangements, opening hours and care. The dramatic section is memorable, but the rules about who can use which space are part of the architecture too.</p>
    <h2>A roof can be somebody else’s ground.</h2>
    <p>Treat the raised court as a lesson in connection, not a free bonus parcel. Getting people up to it, keeping the depot working, isolating noise and vibration, and managing structure and drainage all have consequences. A complicated site can produce a wonderful place without being cheap or easy to repeat.</p>
    <p>For a Berkeley version, start with a ground-level alternative. What is gained by stacking the uses, and does that gain justify elevators, loading, waterproofing and long-term repairs? A great section still needs a reserve fund.</p>
    <aside class="research-note mt-8">Reading lens: project publications document the place and its intended design. They are not independent evaluations of affordability, energy performance or how every resident experiences life there. The questions here are our interpretation.</aside>
  </section>

